lessons.body:
Two components may need to display and update the same information. Giving each a separate `useState` variable creates two independent copies, not shared state. If they must agree, choose a single owner and pass the value down through props.

**Lifting state up** moves state to the closest common parent of the components that need it. The parent owns the value; children receive it plus a **callback prop** to request changes. Data travels down, and events travel up through function calls. A child does not edit its props or reach into a sibling.

## Two editors, one value

The following component functions assume `useState` is imported. `Editor` has no local state for this value. Its `label` prop supplies a visible name and its `onChange` prop accepts the next string, not the browser event.

```jsx
function Editor({
  label, value, onChange
}) {
  return (
    <label>
      {label}
      <input value={value}
        onChange={event =>
          onChange(event.target.value)} />
    </label>
  );
}
function SharedEditors() {
  const [text, setText] = useState('');
  return (
    <>
      <Editor label="First" value={text}
        onChange={setText} />
      <Editor label="Second" value={text}
        onChange={setText} />
      <p>{text}</p>
    </>
  );
}
```

Type “Hello” into First. Its handler calls `setText('Hello')` through the prop. The parent receives the update and renders again. Both editors now receive “Hello”, and the paragraph displays it too. Editing Second follows exactly the same route. No extra synchronisation step is necessary because only one string is stored.

## Controlled is a design relationship

An application component is often described as a **controlled component** when its important behaviour is determined by props. Here the parent controls the editors' text. A component can still have unrelated local state, such as whether a help message is open. Controlled and uncontrolled are useful design descriptions, not strict categories imposed on every custom component.

Lift only as high as necessary. If no other component needs a temporary draft, keeping it local makes the relationship easier to understand. Moving every variable to the top of the application makes distant components responsible for details they do not use.

Do not automatically initialise a child's state from a prop and expect it to follow later changes. `useState(initialValue)` uses that initial value when the component first mounts; subsequent prop updates do not overwrite the stored state. Keep the value controlled, or deliberately design an independent draft and its reset behaviour.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: Two displays, one thermostat
> Two wall displays show one thermostat setting. Each sends adjustment requests to the same controller.
>
> **How it maps:** The parent is the owner of the setting; props show the current value and callback props request updates from either child.
>
> **Where it stops:** React does not require physical messages or a central owner for all data. Independent settings can stay in independent components.

## Key takeaways
- Shared values need one owner at the nearest useful common parent.
- Pass current data down and change-request callbacks to children.
- Keep independent data local; do not mirror props into state by default.

## Further reading
- [Sharing state between components](https://react.dev/learn/sharing-state-between-components)
- [Choosing state structure](https://react.dev/learn/choosing-the-state-structure)

State should store information that must be remembered between renders. If a value can be calculated from current props and state, usually calculate it during render instead. **Derived state** such as a filtered list or full name does not need a separate setter merely because it appears on screen.

Every duplicated value creates another update that you might forget. A search box with `query`, `tasks` and a separately stored `visibleTasks` can become inconsistent when tasks change but the filtered copy does not.

## Derive a filtered result

This component-body excerpt assumes `useState` is imported and `tasks` is an array prop containing objects with string `title` fields. The query starts empty. `toLowerCase` returns lower-case text. The string method `String.prototype.includes`, used as `title.includes(needle)` below, tests whether the title string contains the search text. The callback has a block body, so its result needs an explicit `return`.

```js
const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
const needle = query.toLowerCase();
const visibleTasks = tasks.filter(task => {
  const title = task.title.toLowerCase();
  return title.includes(needle);
});
const visibleCount = visibleTasks.length;
```

With “Read React” and “Buy tea”, the query “READ” produces just “Read React”, and the count is 1. If the parent later removes that task, the next render recalculates an empty result. There is no second stored list to repair. The original task array is unchanged.

The calculation belongs in render because it only reads inputs and produces a result. It does not contact a server, change the DOM directly, or request state updates. For a small list, start with this simple calculation; performance tools and optional caching belong later.

## Avoid impossible combinations

A submit workflow can be idle, submitting, successful or failed. Four booleans allow contradictory combinations, such as successful and failed simultaneously. One status string restricts the current mode to one chosen value. This component-body excerpt defines the initial status:

```js
const [status, setStatus] = useState(
  'idle'
);
const isBusy = status === 'submitting';
```

Use `setStatus('submitting')` when starting an operation and an appropriate terminal status when it finishes. This does not start or finish a submission by itself; it supplies a representation that is easier to reason about. An error message can still be stored separately if the application needs to remember its content.

When selecting one task, prefer storing its ID over a duplicate task object. Find the current object from the task array during render. If the ID is absent after deletion, handle that case explicitly, for example by showing no selection. This avoids a selected copy silently keeping an old title while the main list has a new one.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: One bill, calculated totals
> A receipt stores its line items and calculates the total from them. Keeping a second editable total invites disagreement after an item changes.
>
> **How it maps:** Tasks and query are the inputs; the filtered result and count are calculations from those inputs.
>
> **Where it stops:** Some values really are independent records, such as a historical price agreed earlier. Only derive values whose meaning is determined by current inputs.

## Key takeaways
- Derive display values from current inputs during render.
- Represent modes that cannot be active together with one status value.
- Store an identity rather than a duplicate object when selecting existing data.

## Further reading
- [Choosing state structure](https://react.dev/learn/choosing-the-state-structure)
- [Reacting to input with state](https://react.dev/learn/reacting-to-input-with-state)

React associates state with a component's identity in the rendered tree. Rendering a fresh JSX expression does not necessarily create fresh state. When the same component type stays in the same position with the same key, React normally preserves its state through updates.

A **remount** replaces a component instance with a new one and starts its local state again. Removing a component, changing its type at that place, or changing its key can discard the old state. Consider the resulting tree, not the location of the JSX text in a file.

## A draft for a recipient

These component functions assume `useState` is imported. The parent supplies `recipientId` and `recipientName` as props. This example keeps a local draft while the same recipient remains selected; it does not implement a submit action or contact a recipient.

```jsx
function Draft({ name }) {
  const [text, setText] = useState('');
  return (
    <label>
      Message to {name}
      <input value={text}
        onChange={event =>
          setText(event.target.value)} />
    </label>
  );
}
function Chat({
  recipientId, recipientName
}) {
  return (
    <Draft key={recipientId}
      name={recipientName} />
  );
}
```

Type a draft for recipient 7. Updating only the name of recipient 7 leaves the key and component type unchanged, so the draft survives. Switching to recipient 8 changes the key: React removes the old Draft and creates a fresh one with an empty string. Switching back to 7 creates another fresh Draft; a key does not cache removed state.

Without a key that changes, changing the `name` prop alone would keep the existing draft at this position. That can be useful for cosmetic prop changes and dangerous when the draft belongs to a different person. Decide whether preserving or resetting matches the interaction.

## Stable keys and stable definitions

Keys distinguish siblings within their parent, not components throughout the whole application. A task's stable ID is usually suitable. An array index can attach a row's local state to the wrong task after insertion or reordering. A random key generated on every render forces replacement repeatedly, losing input state rather than fixing identity.

Define component functions at the top level of their module. Defining a child component inside a parent's function creates a different function object on every parent render. React sees a different component type and can reset the child's state unexpectedly. Move the child definition outside and pass needed data as props.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: A labelled cubby
> Belongings for a child stay in the same labelled cubby. Reassigning that cubby to a different child means clearing it before use.
>
> **How it maps:** The location and label help model component identity; changing a key requests a fresh state holder in that parent.
>
> **Where it stops:** Keys are local to siblings and do not store discarded state somewhere else. Returning to an old key does not retrieve an archived draft.

## Key takeaways
- State follows identity in the tree, using position, type and key.
- Change a key deliberately when a new entity needs fresh local state.
- Stable IDs and top-level component definitions prevent accidental resets.

## Further reading
- [Preserving and resetting state](https://react.dev/learn/preserving-and-resetting-state)
- [Rendering lists](https://react.dev/learn/rendering-lists)

Several handlers may update the same related state. A **reducer** gathers those update rules in one pure function. It receives the current state and a **reducer action**, then returns the next state. An action describes what happened; by convention it is an object with a `type` string plus any data needed for that event.

`useReducer` is an alternative to `useState`, not a global store. Each mounted component instance has its own reducer state, preserved across its renders. It is useful when several events must follow consistent rules, while a simple text field may remain clearer with `useState`.

## A pure reducer

This complete JavaScript function needs no React import. The object spread preserves other fields while replacing `count`. The **switch statement** selects a branch from `action.type`; the **throw statement** reports an unexpected action instead of silently losing the state.

```js
function counterReducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'added':
      return {
        ...state,
        count: state.count + action.amount
      };
    case 'reset':
      return { ...state, count: 0 };
    default:
      throw Error('Unknown action');
  }
}
```

The reducer must not mutate its inputs, send network requests or produce random identifiers. It calculates the next value from the given state and action. If an event needs an ID or the current time, create that event information in the handler and put it in the action. Then repeated reducer evaluation produces the same result.

## Connect an event to the reducer

This component function assumes `useReducer` is imported from React and the reducer above is in scope. Call `useReducer` at the top level of the component. **Dispatch** is the function React returns for requesting a transition.

```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(
    counterReducer, { count: 0 }
  );
  return (
    <>
      <p>{state.count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({
        type: 'added', amount: 2
      })}>Add two</button>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({
        type: 'reset'
      })}>Reset</button>
    </>
  );
}
```

Clicking Add two dispatches `{ type: 'added', amount: 2 }`. React passes that action and current state to the reducer. Starting at 0, two separate Add two clicks produce 2, then 4. Reset produces 0. The component displays each resulting state; the handler does not assign to `state.count` directly.

Dispatch requests a future update, so code after `dispatch` still reads its render's existing snapshot. In development Strict Mode, React may call a reducer twice to expose impurities and ignore one result. Keep reducers pure and test state transitions rather than assuming an exact call count.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: A scorekeeper with event cards
> A scorekeeper follows written rules for “add two” and “reset” cards. The same score and card always produce the same next score.
>
> **How it maps:** The current score is state, a card is an action, and the rulebook is the reducer. Dispatch submits an event for React to process.
>
> **Where it stops:** The reducer itself must only calculate. It does not physically update a display, send messages or create event information.

## Key takeaways
- Reducers centralise related update rules as pure transitions.
- Actions describe events; dispatch asks React to apply one.
- Return new state, preserve required fields and keep external work in handlers.

## Further reading
- [Extracting state logic into a reducer](https://react.dev/learn/extracting-state-logic-into-a-reducer)
- [useReducer reference](https://react.dev/reference/react/useReducer)
